Brute force I came up with ---
So here i sorted the array and just return the second last element from the array .
The problem is there can be duplicates in the array so we have to get unique array.
To do that i used unique function moves all unique elements to the front of the range. It returns an iterator (newEnd) pointing to the logical new end of the unique elements.
Elements after newEnd still exist in memory, but their values are unspecified.
auto newEnd = unique(nums.begin(), nums.end());
after that i erased the duplicate elements that were accumalated in the end of the array.
nums.erase(newEnd, nums.end());
also if the array contains only 1 element or all elements are same there would be no second largest element return -1 in that case.
code---
class Solution {
public:
    int secondLargestElement(vector<int>& nums) {
    sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());
    auto newEnd = unique(nums.begin(), nums.end());
    nums.erase(newEnd, nums.end());
    if(nums.size()<2){
        return -1;
    }
    return nums[nums.size()-2];
    }
};
time complexity here is O(nlogn)

The most optimal approach ---
would be using two variables One largest and other second largest and traversing the array if largest is less than a[i] changes value of largest
to a[i] and value of second largest to largest .
after finding the largest if largest is greater than a[i] but a[i] is greater than second largest changes second largest to a[i]
also have to check for the case where the second largest value doesn't exist;
code---
class Solution {
public:
    int secondLargestElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        int largest = INT_MIN;
        int secondLargest = INT_MIN;

        for(int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if(nums[i] > largest) {
                secondLargest = largest;
                largest = nums[i];
            }
            else if(nums[i] > secondLargest && nums[i] < largest) {
                secondLargest = nums[i];
            }
        }

        if(secondLargest == INT_MIN)
            return -1;

        return secondLargest;
    }
};

Time complexity here is O(n)


